{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261126T043100Z-1c3a1af2", "ts": "2026-11-26T04:35:03Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.10.0", "branch": "fix/agent-runnable-doctor-janitor", "build": "e9c06c071287"}, "body": {"base_commit": "a84fedfc1f142cbb6312b380af0bbe1a7819202a", "base_tree": "106b51d5f1d6749e71a1740883c5dbe6fb6e22d7", "head_tree": "da226f87ce35de85ed0d87946cafdf3ae2dd84ac", "head_commit": "442b05da662d60c6436d788210dbf8ae38d98f34", "dispatch_commit": "442b05da662d60c6436d788210dbf8ae38d98f34", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "rule-4 cumulative: working tree is clean, so chunk/final would dispatch an empty interval; branch is 1 commit(s) ahead of origin/develop and no cumulative record covers current HEAD \u2014 the committed bundle is cumulative's scope", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/janitor/SKILL.md", "tests/test_backlog_skill_metadata.py", "tests/test_skill_command_grants.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/changes/agent-runnable-doctor-janitor.md", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/janitor/SKILL.md", "tests/test_backlog_skill_metadata.py", "tests/test_skill_command_grants.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/changes/agent-runnable-doctor-janitor.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "Test evidence is stale: it predates the session and was recorded on a tree that is neither base nor head", "recommendation": "`prawduct-hook test-status` exits 1 with `stale: evidence predates session (2026-11-26T02:42:59Z < 2026-11-26T04:08:24Z)`. `.test-evidence.json` records `evidence_tree` 3f1228ab, but the reviewed head tree is da226f87 and the base tree is 106b51d5. So no recorded run covers the three new tests in tests/test_backlog_skill_metadata.py, the emptied `_WRITE_REACH_EXEMPT`, or `test_every_instructed_command_is_granted` meeting doctor's new `plan bind` instruction. Run the suite through `prawduct-hook test-evidence record` (`testament run`, per documentation/running-tests.md) before the PR. The probe also printed a plugin-binary-skew NOTE: it ran prawduct-live's binary, not this checkout's. Instance.", "files": ["tests/test_backlog_skill_metadata.py", "tests/test_skill_command_grants.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "The one evidence record, which covers neither this commit's tree nor its base.", "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The new no-interpreter rail recognises only `<named interpreter> *`, so it passes model-invocable grants that run anything, including the backlog-import bypass this change closed", "recommendation": "`test_no_model_invocable_skill_pre_approves_an_interpreter` says a model-invocable skill must not grant an interpreter whole. But `_INTERPRETER_GRANT` (`^(python3?|node|npm|\u2026)? ?\\*$`) matches only a bare interpreter name followed by a star. Under the repo's own `_grant_matches` semantics, three model-invocable grants still admit arbitrary execution and pass both this rail and `test_no_model_invocable_skill_grants_an_irreversible_op`, which only matches commands that start with `prawduct-hook`: runbook's `Bash(find *)` admits `find . -maxdepth 0 -exec prawduct-hook backlog import \u2026 \\;`; pr's `Bash(git *)` admits `git -c alias.x='!prawduct-hook backlog import \u2026' x`; and onboard's `Bash(git -C * status*)` lets the star absorb `-c core.fsmonitor=<cmd>`. A maintainer who adds `Bash(uv run *)`, `Bash(bash -c*)` or `Bash(xargs *)` to a model-invocable skill also stays green, while the test's docstring and the change record ('an interpreter reaches every op an allow-list withholds') say that class is guarded. Why it broke: the predicate lists interpreter names instead of asking whether a grant's wildcard can carry an exec-capable argument. Class, with members outside the diff. Close it by construction: one predicate over 'a grant whose wildcard reaches an exec-capable program or flag' (find -exec/-execdir/-ok, xargs, env, a git star placed before the subcommand or bare `git *`, `uv run`/`npx`/`bash -c` forms), with per-skill exemption rows that give a reason (pr's push needs `git *`) and red controls for each shape. Otherwise, narrow the docstring and the change record's claim to 'named interpreters'.", "files": ["tests/test_backlog_skill_metadata.py"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A Bash grant in a model-invocable skill or agent whose wildcard can carry an exec-capable argument or program, so it admits a command the allow-list was meant to withhold.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/skills/runbook/SKILL.md allowed-tools Bash(find *)"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md allowed-tools Bash(git *)"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md allowed-tools Bash(git -C * status*) and its -C * add/commit siblings"}, {"id": "m4", "site": "tests/test_backlog_skill_metadata.py _INTERPRETER_GRANT"}], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Destructive-git reach is fenced for janitor only; pr, also model-invocable, still pre-approves `Bash(git *)` with no recorded reason", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke, in one sentence: the change record's reason for narrowing janitor ('would then have pre-approved `git push --force`, `git reset --hard` and `git branch -D` for an agent') is a property of every model-invocable skill's git grant, but the new rail `test_janitor_git_grant_admits_no_destructive_verb` is hard-wired to janitor. On HEAD, `plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md` (`disable-model-invocation: false`) grants `Bash(git *)`, which `_grant_matches` confirms admits every entry in `_DESTRUCTIVE_GIT`. No test, artifact or frontmatter comment records why pr is exempt (critic's equivalent is pinned separately, in tests/test_critic_skill_metadata.py). A future skill that adds `Bash(git *)` passes every rail. Per core.md, 'A review finding names an instance; the defect is a class... fix the class through one owner.' Fix it by construction: make the git rail sweep every model-invocable skill, the way `test_no_model_invocable_skill_pre_approves_an_interpreter` does, with an exemption map in the `_WRITE_REACH_EXEMPT` shape that carries pr's reason for the verbs it needs (push, merge, rebase). Narrowing pr's grant instead is also acceptable. Either way, delete the janitor-only special case, or fold it into the sweep.", "files": ["tests/test_backlog_skill_metadata.py", "plugin/skills/janitor/SKILL.md"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A model-invocable skill whose Bash grants match any command in `_DESTRUCTIVE_GIT` with no recorded exemption.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md allowed-tools `Bash(git *)`"}], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "rule-unenforced: doctor now runs repairs itself but has no instruction for when they fail (plan bind refused:, archive-plan not-archived:, coverage-scaffold's exit-0 'NOTE: could not write')", "recommendation": "This rests on the authoring learning \"Instructions that drive code are sourced from the CODE's surface \u2014 read the handler; give every state, refusal code, envelope field and failure mode a line\". Before this change the owner ran each --apply and saw its output. Now the agent runs it, and three of the newly agent-run commands can fail with no line in doctor saying what to do. (1) Check #24 now says to run `prawduct-hook plan bind <plan> --branch <name>`. Its handler (cmd plan bind, plugin/bin/prawduct-hook ~8853) exits 1 with `refused: <code>: <detail>` (plan_mint.PlanMintRefused: branch-is-base, write-failed, ...) or prints `unchanged:`. Doctor holds Edit and Write, so an agent that gets a refusal and has no instruction will plausibly hand-edit the plan's `branch:` line. That works around a refusal the diff deliberately stopped offering as a route when it deleted 'by hand'. (2) Check #24 and the Lifecycle Convergence Flow now run `archive-plan` on the owner's yes. cmd_archive_plan exits 1 with `not-archived: <reason>`, and doctor gives no line for it. (3) Check #11 now runs `coverage-scaffold --apply`. Its loop prints `NOTE: could not write <dest>` to stderr and still exits 0. Nothing in doctor tells the agent to relay that line, so the agent will tell the owner the stubs exist and that filling them is the owner's job when some were never written. Scope is a class: any apply this change moved from the owner to the agent whose failure output its check gives no line. Fix each member with one sentence in its check naming the failure output and saying 'relay it, nothing else changed; do not edit the file by hand'. This is filed once as rule-unenforced because the learning is written and no test checks that a skill instructing a hook command covers that command's refusal outputs. Mechanising it would need a test that, for each hook subcommand a model-invocable skill tells the agent to run with a write (`--apply`, `bind`, `archive-plan`), asserts the skill text names the handler's refusal prefix (`refused:`, `not-archived:`, `NOTE: could not write`). Those prefixes could come from a small per-command registry next to the handlers.", "files": ["plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md"], "check": "rule-unenforced", "scope": "class", "closure": "A repair command that doctor's prose now tells the agent (not the owner) to run, whose handler can refuse or partially fail, and whose doctor check gives that failure output no line.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md Check #24 \u2014 prawduct-hook plan bind (refused: <code>: <detail>, exit 1)"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md Check #24 and Lifecycle Convergence Flow \u2014 prawduct-hook archive-plan (not-archived: <reason>, exit 1)"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md Check #11 \u2014 prawduct-hook coverage-scaffold --apply (NOTE: could not write <dest>, exit 0)"}], "fid": "R-4"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 4, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "`_skill_bash_patterns` reads `!Bash(...)` deny entries as grants", "recommendation": "The regex `Bash\\(([^)]*)\\)` also captures critic's `!Bash(pytest*)`/`!Bash(* python -m pytest*)` denials. This errs toward false positives, so today it hides nothing. But a skill that does the safe thing and denies `!Bash(python3 *)` would fail the interpreter rail, and a `!Bash(git push*)` deny on janitor would fail the destructive-git rail. Skip entries preceded by `!` if the helper is touched again. No edit needed for this PR.", "files": ["tests/test_backlog_skill_metadata.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Janitor's narrowed git grant leaves common read verbs ungranted (rev-parse, branch --list, worktree list, stash list)", "recommendation": "These will prompt in default mode. That looks deliberate and right: `git branch*`, `git worktree*` and `git stash*` prefixes would also admit `-D`/`remove`/`drop`, and the survey's branch and worktree listing goes through `git-tidy`, which is granted. The frontmatter says 'the read verbs the survey uses'. themes.md names no git command besides git-tidy, so that claim is not falsifiable. No edit wanted.", "files": ["plugin/skills/janitor/SKILL.md"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation: nothing to reconcile", "recommendation": "No plan claims this branch (scope unbound), the change record carries no `governed_by:`, and record_lint reports governed-by-gap 0. security-model.md has no Direction about skill grants or model invocation. The doctor prose still requires the owner's yes before every `--apply` and keeps the one-confirmation rule in the Lifecycle Convergence Flow. The CLI forms the new prose instructs exist: `archive-plan --state superseded --superseded-by`, and `plan bind <plan> --branch`, whose `plan bind*` grant extends to no other plan op (new|bind|resolve|status|import-status|retract|check-claims).", "files": ["plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "`_INTERPRETER_GRANT` sees an interpreter by an enumerated name list, so the absence pin misses executors under any other spelling", "recommendation": "The regex matches only `<listed name> *` or a bare `*`. So `Bash(uv run *)`, `Bash(npm run *)`, `Bash(npx --yes *)`, `Bash(make *)`, `Bash(env *)` and `Bash(xargs *)` all pass `test_no_model_invocable_skill_pre_approves_an_interpreter` while granting arbitrary execution. No current skill carries one, so nothing is wrong today. If the pin is meant to hold the class, key it on the property ('a literal prefix before the first `*` that names an executor, or none') rather than on a list of names. Per core.md, 'A coverage, completeness or absence claim... holds only when its falsifying query returns nothing \u2014 query the concept in more than your own words'.", "files": ["tests/test_backlog_skill_metadata.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "`_skill_bash_patterns` reads `!Bash(...)` deny rules as grants, and re-implements the parse already inlined in the irreversible-op rail", "recommendation": "The regex `Bash\\(([^)]*)\\)` also captures deny entries. Critic's `!Bash(pytest*)` and its siblings are returned as patterns today. They match neither new rail, so the effect is latent, and it would show up as a loud false positive: a skill that denies `!Bash(python3 *)` or `!Bash(git push*)` would fail as having granted it. While you are in this file, have `test_no_model_invocable_skill_grants_an_irreversible_op` call the new helper instead of keeping its own copy of the same two lines, and make the helper skip `!`-prefixed entries.", "files": ["tests/test_backlog_skill_metadata.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Janitor's git grant is a hand list of 'the read verbs the survey uses', and common survey reads fall outside it", "recommendation": "The themes never name git verbs, so the list is not derived from anything. `git branch -a`, `git worktree list`, `git rev-parse`, `git rev-list`, `git remote -v` and `git stash list` are natural VCS-hygiene reads, and in default mode they will now prompt. The consumer note accepts prompts for non-read verbs, so this is a friction note, not a defect. Add whichever of these the survey plausibly needs, or soften the frontmatter comment's 'the survey uses' to 'the read verbs granted'. No edit is required.", "files": ["plugin/skills/janitor/SKILL.md"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The boundary doc still describes doctor's repairs as 'each owner-invoked' and doctor as 'reports and guides rather than fixes'", "recommendation": "plugin/docs/doctor-vs-janitor.md line 24 ('the bounded `prawduct-hook` operations it drives, each owner-invoked') and janitor SKILL.md line 44 ('reports-and-guides rather than fixes') now read slightly off: doctor runs the repairs itself once the owner says yes. Before this change, update-gitignore and evidence configure already worked that way, so 'owner-invoked' can be read as 'owner-approved', and nothing anyone does changes. 'Owner-approved' would be the exact word if the row is ever touched. No edit is wanted for this branch.", "files": ["plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/janitor/SKILL.md"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Shipped test comments narrate the janitor grant's history", "recommendation": "Three passages narrate history. In tests/test_skill_command_grants.py: the module docstring's '(The interpreter grant is gone: janitor became model-invocable...)' and the `_WRITE_REACH_EXEMPT` comment's 'Its one row was janitor's...'. In tests/test_backlog_skill_metadata.py: the irreversible-op docstring's 'Janitor was then made human-invoked... it is model-invocable again'. The protocol's prose remedy for history narration in a shipped comment is deletion: state the current rule, for example '_WRITE_REACH_EXEMPT: grants allowed to reach a write unnamed, with reasons; none today'. This is prose that nothing reads, so it stays an observation.", "files": ["tests/test_skill_command_grants.py", "tests/test_backlog_skill_metadata.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "The test docstring cites 'The owner's ruling' as authority for making janitor model-invocable, but no committed record holds that ruling", "recommendation": "test_doctor_and_janitor_are_model_invocable opens with 'The owner's ruling: an agent may run both'. The change fragment records the reasoning (why the interpreter grants went and why the fence is no longer needed) but not that the owner asked for it. The only record of the ask is the gitignored handoff notes. This reverses 85753fda's recorded decision to fence janitor at the skill. The core learning \"A governance change cannot supply its own authority\" asks for the owner's confirmation to land somewhere the amendment isn't. Put the owner's ask in the PR description, or add one clause to the fragment, so the docstring's citation can be checked. The safety reasoning itself holds: no interpreter grant means the backlog import/merge ops still prompt, and the durable CLI gate is still tracked as BKL-8H2W.", "files": ["tests/test_backlog_skill_metadata.py", ".prawduct/changes/agent-runnable-doctor-janitor.md"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Janitor's git grant is described as 'the read verbs the survey uses', but the survey prose names no git verbs, so the list is the builder's own choice", "recommendation": "Read verbs a VCS-hygiene survey plausibly reaches for (`git branch -a/--merged`, `git worktree list`, `git rev-list`, `git stash list`) are not granted, so in default mode they prompt. The janitor frontmatter already notes, for cache-query, that a declined prompt looks to janitor exactly like an unreadable source. The consumer note does say other git commands prompt, and branch and worktree listing is covered by the granted git-tidy, so this changes little. Mentioned so the correctness reviewer can decide whether any survey step depends on an ungranted read.", "files": ["plugin/skills/janitor/SKILL.md"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass over files_oracle {.prawduct/changes/agent-runnable-doctor-janitor.md}: no bar cleared", "recommendation": "Checked each claim in the fragment against the diff. Janitor's python3/npm grants are deleted, git is narrowed to the nine listed verbs, hook-subcommand grants are unchanged, the plan bind grant is added, the _WRITE_REACH_EXEMPT row is gone, and the three new tests exist. The edits doctor must not make (.gitattributes in #17, operator-verification, plugin caches in #21) are still left to the owner. The consumer note is accurate. One imprecision clears no bar: 'archive-plan and plan-claim repairs now read: show the dry run ... run the `--apply` yourself'. archive-plan writes when invoked and plan bind has no dry run or --apply, and doctor does not show a dry run for either. Doctor's own prose, not the fragment, is what directs the agent, so nobody acts wrongly on it. No edit wanted.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/agent-runnable-doctor-janitor.md"], "oid": "O-11"}], "duration_seconds": 480, "scope": null, "scope_chosen_by": "none", "scope_unresolved_cause": "unbound", "scope_reason": "no live plan claims `fix/agent-runnable-doctor-janitor`", "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunks_declared": false, "chunks_evidence": {}, "base_reviewed": "a84fedfc1f142cbb6312b380af0bbe1a7819202a", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/changes/agent-runnable-doctor-janitor.md", "plugin/skills/doc